\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Econometrics II: Exam 2 Solutions (2012)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a 
\emph on
Hausman-Taylor
\emph default
 panel data model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=w_{i1}^{\prime}\gamma_{1}+w_{i2}^{\prime}\gamma_{2}+x_{it1}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+x_{it2}^{\prime}\beta_{2}+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T;\ i=1,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the unobserved hetoergeneity and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the structural error.
 The regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

 are time-variant.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i1}c_{i})=E(x_{it1}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 might be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it2}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose we don't observe variables other than 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it2}$
\end_inset

.
 Propose an estimator of parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\gamma_{1},\gamma_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Express the estimator in terms of observables.
 be specific about other assumptions you may need.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we must assume strict exogeneity conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{it}|w_{i},x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption, along with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i1}c_{i})=E(x_{it1}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, provide orthogonality conditions that can be used in a method of moments
 procedure.
 Next, let us stack the panel data model over the 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=W_{i}\gamma+X_{i}\beta+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 are strictly exogenous and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}v_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}=I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is the time-demeaning matrix, it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\left(Q_{T}X_{i}\right)^{\prime}v_{i}\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}X_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be used as instruments in estimation.
 Additional instruments come from 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i1}c_{i})=E(x_{it1}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is orthogonal to 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and so is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $TK_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

 vector containing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it1}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, we have the set of instrumets 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{it}\equiv\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\ddot{x}_{it} & w_{i1} & x_{i1}^{o}\end{array}\right]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 With this set of instruments, the order condition for identificaiton of
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\gamma,\beta)$
\end_inset

 is that 
\begin_inset Formula $TK_{1}\geq J_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 In effect, we must have a sufficient number of elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}^{o}$
\end_inset

 to act as instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 are the IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i1}$
\end_inset

 act as their own IVs).
 Now, we can obtain consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\gamma,\beta)$
\end_inset

 by implementing a P2SLS procedure.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\Lambda} & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}g_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}g_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}g_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}z_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}z_{it}y_{it}\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{it}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
w_{i} & x_{it}\end{array}\right]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\gamma & \beta\end{array}\right]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the 
\emph on
correlated random effects probit
\emph default
 model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{it}=1|x_{i},c_{i})=\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the unobserved heterogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the structural error, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

 is the CDF of the standard normal distribution, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=(x_{i1}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{iT}^{\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 has exponential heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive and explain the 
\emph on
attenuation bias
\emph default
 of the pooled MLE estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Propose an estimator of parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta^{\prime},\gamma^{\prime},\xi^{\prime},\sigma_{c}^{2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Express the estimator in terms of observables.
 Be specific about other assumptions you may need.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the latent variable model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{it}^{*} & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}\\
y_{it} & = & 1[y_{it}^{*}>0]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}|x_{it},c_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 being unobserved, define 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=u_{it}+c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}|x_{it},c_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}^{*}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+v_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{it}^{*}>0|x_{i},c_{i})\\
 & = & P(v_{it}>-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta|x_{i},c_{i})\\
 & = & P(v_{it}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma>-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma|x_{i},c_{i})\\
 & = & P\left(\left.\frac{v_{it}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}<\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}\right|x_{i},c_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the attenuation bias of the pooled MLE estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}\rightarrow\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}
\]

\end_inset


\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the unobserved effects probit model, we specify a response probability
 that fully determines the conditional distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})=\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})
\]

\end_inset

We need to assume
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strict Exogeneity (SE): 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=D(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is a conditional distribution.
 Under strict exogeneity assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 only depends on the current time period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Conditional Independence (CI): 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}$
\end_inset

 are independent conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the latent variable model,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}^{*}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it};\ y_{it}=1\left[y_{it}^{*}>0\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}|x_{it},c_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{i},c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

, and note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{it}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the given assumptions, conditional maximum likelihood approach is
 available for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta^{\prime},\gamma^{\prime},\xi^{\prime},\sigma_{c}^{2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not observed, it cannot appear in the likelihood function.
 Instead, we find the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, a step that requires us to integrate out 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi))$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i};\theta) & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}f(y_{i},c_{i}|x_{i})\ dc_{i}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},c_{i})\cdot f(c_{i}|x_{i})\ dc_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

By conditional independence and strict exogeneity, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},a_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}f(y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}f(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})
\]

\end_inset

and from part A,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},c_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}\Phi(w_{it})^{y_{it}}\left[1-\Phi(w_{it})\right]^{1-y_{it}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}\equiv\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, given the distrubtion of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(c_{i}|x_{i})=\left(\nicefrac{1}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}\right)\phi\left[\nicefrac{c_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{c}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi)}}\right]\equiv h(c_{i},\sigma_{c},\bar{x}_{i},\xi)
\]

\end_inset

Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i};\theta)={\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}\left\{ {\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}\Phi(w_{it})^{y_{it}}\left[1-\Phi(w_{it})\right]^{1-y_{it}}\right\} \cdot h(c_{i},\sigma_{c},\bar{x}_{i},\xi)\ dc_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Taking the log of the equation above gives the conditional log likelihood
 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 contains 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta^{\prime},\gamma^{\prime},\xi^{\prime},\sigma_{c}^{2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the panel data model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the uobserved heterogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the structural error.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is of interest and is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $T\geq3$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Describe the standard 
\emph on
fixed effects 
\emph default
(FE) estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

.
 Be specific about other assumptions you may need.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Discuss the advantage of using the FE estimator over the RE (random effects)
 estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How to calculate the robust/non-robust standard errors for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

? Discuss what the robustness means.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Propose an FE estimator that is more efficient than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the panel data model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unobserved heterogeneity and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is idiosyncratic error.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is of interest and is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $T\geq3$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is strictly exogenous, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

 and time varying.
 However, we allow 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})\neq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One way to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the fixed effects method.
 First we average the regression equation over 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{y}_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{i}=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum}_{t=1}^{T}y_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Subtracting this cross seciton equation from the panel data equation for
 each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 gives the FE transformed equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ddot{y}_{it}=\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{it}=y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}=x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{\eta}_{it}=\eta_{it}-\bar{\eta}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the fixed-effects transformation eliminates the unobserved effect
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(x_{it},\eta_{is})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{u}_{it})=E\left[(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i})\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 In order to ensure the FE estimator is well behaved asymptotically, we
 also need a standard rank condition on the matrix of time-demeanded expalantory
 variables, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{rank}\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime})\right)=K
\]

\end_inset

Under the these two assumptions, we can obtain the consistent FE estimator,
 which is the POLS estimator from the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{it}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{FE}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{y}_{it}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The RE approach to estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 effectively puts 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 into the error term, under the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is orthogonal to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and then accounts for the implied serial correlation in the composite
 error 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

 using a GLS analysis.
 In many applications, the whole point of using panel data is to allow for
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be arbitrarily correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 A fixed effects analysis achieves this purpose explicitly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can write the FE transformed equation for all time periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ddot{y}_{i}=\ddot{X}_{i}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{X}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

.
 This set of equations is obtained by pre-multiplying
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+j_{T}c_{i}+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

by a time-demeaning matrix.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}\equiv I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, which is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 symmetric, idempotent matrix with rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 Further, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}j_{T}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}=Q_{T}y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{X}_{i}=Q_{T}X_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 So, the FE estimator can be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{FE} & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \beta+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}=\left(Q_{T}X_{i}\right)^{\prime}\left(Q_{T}u_{i}\right)=X_{i}^{\prime}Q_{T}Q_{T}u_{i}=X_{i}^{\prime}Q_{T}u_{i}=\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}$
\end_inset

, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\\
\sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right) & = & \left(\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

By the CLT,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,\left[E(\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i})\right]^{-1}E(\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i})\left[E(\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i})\right]^{-1}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the robust variance matrix estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

, which is valid in the presence of any heteroskedasticy or serial correlation
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=\left(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)\left(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}-\ddot{X}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

.
 The robust standard errors are obtained as the square roots of the diagonal
 elements of this matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, if we assume spherical conditional homoskedasticity, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}
\]

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,\sigma_{u}^{2}\left[E(\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{X}_{i})\right]^{-1}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

 can be computed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2}) & = & E\left[(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i})^{2}\right]\\
 & = & E(u_{it}^{2})+E(\bar{u}_{i}^{2})-2E(u_{it}\bar{u}_{i})\\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}+\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}-2\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}\\
 & = & \sigma_{u}^{2}\left(1-\frac{1}{T}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If we sum this equation across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, then we can obtain a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=(T-1)\sigma_{u}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{N(T-1)}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=\sigma_{u}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

So, a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\frac{SSR}{\left[N(T-1)-K\right]}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SSR={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the non-robust variance matrix estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}\left(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The FE estimator is only efficient under the spherical conditional homoskedastic
ity assumption, which can fail if heteroskedasticity or serial correlation
 are present in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

.
 Rather than compute a robust variance matrix for the FE estimator, we can
 instead relax spherical conditional homoskedasticity to allow for an unresitrct
ed, albeit constant, conditional covariance matrix.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=\Lambda
\]

\end_inset

a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix.
 Under this assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i})=E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})=Q_{T}\Lambda Q_{T}$
\end_inset

, which has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the variance matrix cannot be inverted unlesswe drop one of the
 time periods from the analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we drop time period 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, leaving equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ddot{y}_{i1} & = & \ddot{x}_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i1}\\
 & \vdots\\
\ddot{y}_{i,T-1} & = & \ddot{x}_{i,T-1}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i,T-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{X}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Also, define the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times(T-1)$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, we estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by FE in the first stage.
 After dropping the last time period for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, define the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}-\ddot{X}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
 A consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\Omega}=\frac{1}{n}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

The fixed effects GLS (FEGLS) estimator is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

For consistency of FEGLS, we assume the rank condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{rank}E\left(\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)=K
\]

\end_inset

The estimated asymptotic variance is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ddot{X}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{X}_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The FEGLS estimator is asymptotically no less efficient than the FE estimator
 under 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=\Lambda$
\end_inset

, even when 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Generally, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda\neq\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

, FEGLS is more efficient than FE, but this conclusion relies on the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, fixed-
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 asymptotics.
\end_layout

